      "label": "Column 1",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "c1-l001",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fadaye."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Carão, ou ſuperficie da carne."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l002",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fafa, l, faua."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Mãy."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fafa` and `faua` give ハハ and the historical variant ハワ for modern 母. The alternation preserves the dictionary's `f`/`u` spelling; Portuguese `Mãy` is modern `mãe`, “mother.”"
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l003",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fafachô, l, fauachô."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Hũa aue aſſi chamada."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fafachô` / `fauachô` gives ハハチョウ / ハワチョウ. The Portuguese supplies only “a bird so called,” so the exact lexical identification should remain open rather than be inferred from the name alone."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l004",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fafô. Noriuo yaburu."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Quebrar aley. Vt."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fafô` reads ハホウ and is naturally 破法. `Noriuo yaburu` is ノリヲ ヤブル, “to break the law,” exactly matching Portuguese `Quebrar a ley`."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l005",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fafôno tomogaraua gigocuni votçuru."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Os"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fafôno tomogaraua gigocuni votçuru` reads ハホウノ トモガラワ ジゴクニ オツル: the lawbreaking people fall into hell. The Portuguese continues on the next physical line."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l006",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "quebrantadores da ley vão ao Inferno."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l007",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fafu."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Fronteſpicio da caſa como angulo que fi-"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fafu` is ハフ, modern 破風, the gable or ornamental end of a Japanese roof. Portuguese describes it as the front or angle at the top of a house."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l008",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "ca no remate das caſas de Iapão."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l009",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagai. Torino tçubaſa."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Aſas dos paſſaros."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Torino tçubaſa` is トリノ ツバサ, “a bird's wings.” The gloss `Aſas dos paſſaros` confirms that `Fagai` names the wings or wing area rather than an unrelated homograph."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l010",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagama."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Panela de ferro coado com hũa cinta"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagama` is ハガマ, modern 羽釜, a cauldron with a projecting iron flange. The Portuguese description continues with the iron band around it."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l011",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "de ferro ao derredor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l012",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagami. i, fauo camu."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " O arreganhar os dẽ-"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagami` is ハガミ, 歯噛み; `fauo camu` is ハヲ カム, “to bite or grind the teeth.” Editorial `i,` introduces that Japanese explanation and is not part of its kana reading."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l013",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "tes agaſtandoſe, ou eſtando dormindo ſem"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l014",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "ſentir, &c. Vt,"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fagamiuo ſuru."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l015",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagmi, u, ǒda."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Rir."
            }
          ],
          "note": "The scan clearly prints `Fagmi`; linguistically this is the verb *fagamu* (modern *hagam(u)*), and NINJAL likewise records the form while noting the probable original misprint for `Fagami`."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l016",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagan."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Vt,"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fagan nixô."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " O ſurrirſe hum"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagan` reads ハガン and is naturally 破顔, the opening of the face into a smile. `Fagan nixô` means to smile; the Portuguese definition completes on the next line."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l017",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "pouco."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Bup"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " (",
              "span_id": "mark",
              "placement": "far-right"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " tardeira.",
              "span_id": "word",
              "placement": "far-right"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l018",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagane."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Aço."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagane` is ハガネ, modern 鋼. Portuguese `Aço` means “steel.”"
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l019",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagaraxi. i, Caraxino fa."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Folhas de mos-"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagaraxi` reads ハガラシ; `Caraxino fa` is カラシノ ハ, “mustard leaves.” Portuguese `mostardeira` is physically interrupted by the displaced `tardeira` printed beside the preceding line."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l020",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagaſumi."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Çujidade dos dentes."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagaſumi` is ハガスミ, dirt or discoloration on the teeth. Historical Portuguese `Çujidade` corresponds to modern `sujidade`."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l021",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagata."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Dentada, ou ſinal de mordedura."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagata` is ハガタ, modern 歯形: a bite or the mark left by biting, as the Portuguese gloss states."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l022",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagaxe, uru, eta."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Fazer eſfolar."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagaxe, uru, eta` gives ハガセル, the causative/transitive sense “to make peel or skin.” The following identically spelled headword has the distinct carpentry sense “to join boards.”"
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l023",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagaxe, uru, eta."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Fazer ajuntar taboas como"
            }
          ],
          "note": "This second `Fagaxe` is distinguished by the Portuguese carpentry gloss. Its example `Touo fagaſuru` on the next line concerns joining the boards of a door, not stripping a surface."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l024",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "fazendo portas, &c. Vt,"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Touo fagaſuru."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l025",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagi."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Afronta, ou vergonha da afronta."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagi` is ハジ, modern 恥. Portuguese gives shame, specifically the shame of an affront; the following lines list constructions with this noun."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l026",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "¶ Fagiuo qiyomuru, l, ſuſugu."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Deſ-"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagiuo qiyomuru` and `Fagiuo ſuſugu` read ハジヲ キヨムル / ススグ, literally to cleanse or rinse away shame. Portuguese `Deſafrontarſe` completes across the line break."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l027",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "afrontarſe. ¶"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fagiuo caquru."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Afron-"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l028",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "tar a alguem. ¶"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fagiuo caqu."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Ficar des-"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l029",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "honrado, ou enuergonhado. ¶"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fagiuo"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l030",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "ſaraſu."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Eſtar à vergonha em publico, &c."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l031",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagi, zzuru, ita."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Ter vergonha. ¶"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fito-"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagi, zzuru, ita` records ハジズル with its inflectional forms. `Fito- / uo fazzuru` is the divided phrase ヒトヲ ハズル, “to feel shame before someone.”"
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l032",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "uo fazzuru."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Ter vergonha, ou pejo dal-"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l033",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "guem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l034",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagifagini."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Aduer. i,"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fazzucaxù."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Ver-"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagifagini` is ハジハジニ, explained by `Fazzucaxù` ハズカシュウ, “shamefully.” The grave accent on final `ù` is a printed feature, not a modernized vowel mark."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l035",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "gonhoſamente."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l036",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagigauaxij."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Ter pejo, ou vergonha."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagigauaxij` reads ハジガワシイ, a historical adjective meaning shameful or embarrassing. Portuguese `pejo` and `vergonha` confirm the sense."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l037",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fagixime, uru, eta."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Enuergonhar a outro."
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fagixime, uru, eta` is ハジシメル, “to shame another.” Historical Portuguese `Enuergonhar` corresponds to modern `envergonhar`."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l038",
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": "Fago."
            },
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": " Varinhas de viſco pera tomar paſſaros"
            }
          ],
          "note": "`Fago` is ハゴ. Its first sense is bird-catching twigs coated with birdlime; the following lines add a children's shuttlecock-like game and connect it with `Fagoita`."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l039",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "¶ Item, Hũa certa fruita ſeca como bugá-"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l040",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "lhos grandes, em que os meninos metem hũas"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l041",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "penas, & como pella rechação com hũa ta-"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c1-l042",
          "indent": 1,
          "runs": [
            {
              "typeface": "italic",
              "text": "boinha que ſe chama Fagoita. ¶"
            },
            {
              "typeface": "roman",
              "text": " Fagiuo"
